/* QuantAI research-methods handbook: built-in answers that work without the AI.
   Each entry: k = keywords/phrases that identify the topic, t = title, c = topic group,
   a = answer (markdown: **bold**, "- " lists, blank line between paragraphs),
   tool = optional [section, tool id, button label] for a QuantAI tool.
   Written by Model Analysis Hub. Add new entries anywhere in the list. */
window.QA_KB_EXTRA = [

/* ---------------- Thesis and proposal ---------------- */
{ c: "thesis", t: "Research proposal", k: ["research proposal", "proposal", "write a proposal", "proposal structure"], a: "A **research proposal** sets out what you will study, why, and how. A typical structure:\n\n- **Title**\n- **Background** and **problem statement**\n- **Research questions / objectives** and hypotheses\n- **Literature review** (brief) and **conceptual framework**\n- **Methodology**: design, setting, population, sampling and sample size, data collection tools, variables, analysis plan\n- **Ethical considerations**\n- **Work plan (timeline)** and **budget**\n- **References** and appendices (questionnaire, consent form)\n\nKeep it specific: a reviewer should be able to picture exactly how the study will run." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Thesis structure (chapters)", k: ["thesis structure", "chapters of a thesis", "thesis chapters", "dissertation structure", "project work structure", "five chapters", "chapter one", "chapter 1"], a: "Most undergraduate and master's theses in Ghana follow five chapters:\n\n- **Chapter 1, Introduction:** background, problem statement, objectives, research questions/hypotheses, significance, scope, limitations, definition of terms.\n- **Chapter 2, Literature review:** themes from previous studies, theoretical and conceptual framework, gaps.\n- **Chapter 3, Methodology:** design, setting, population, sampling, sample size, data collection, variables, analysis, ethics.\n- **Chapter 4, Results:** findings presented in tables and figures, with no interpretation.\n- **Chapter 5, Discussion, conclusions and recommendations:** what the results mean compared with other studies, limitations, conclusions, recommendations.\n\nCheck your department's guidelines: some combine results and discussion." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Background of the study", k: ["background of the study", "background to the study", "introduction chapter", "write the background"], a: "The **background** moves from the general to your specific problem, like a funnel:\n\n- the global picture (with recent statistics, e.g. WHO),\n- the African or regional picture,\n- the Ghana situation (e.g. GSS, Ghana Health Service or DHS figures),\n- your study area and population,\n- what is known and what is still missing, which leads into your problem statement.\n\nEvery claim needs a recent, credible citation. Aim for 1–2 pages." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Problem statement", k: ["problem statement", "statement of the problem", "statement of problem"], a: "A **problem statement** explains, in a few paragraphs, **what is wrong, how we know, and why it matters**:\n\n1. **The ideal:** what should be happening (e.g. national target of 80% immunisation coverage).\n2. **The reality:** what is actually happening, with evidence (e.g. coverage in the district is 62%).\n3. **The gap and its consequences:** who is affected and how.\n4. **What is unknown:** the evidence gap your study fills.\n\nEnd with a sentence that leads to your objective, e.g. \"This study therefore assesses the factors associated with…\"" },
{ c: "thesis", t: "General and specific objectives", k: ["general objective", "specific objectives", "write objectives", "research objectives", "aim and objectives", "aims"], a: "The **general objective (aim)** states the overall purpose in one sentence. **Specific objectives** break it into measurable steps, each starting with an action verb: *to determine, to assess, to identify, to compare, to examine the association between*.\n\nExample:\n- General: To assess hypertension and its associated factors among adults in Ho Municipality.\n- Specific: (1) To determine the prevalence of hypertension; (2) To identify socio-demographic factors associated with hypertension; (3) To assess awareness of hypertension status.\n\nEach specific objective should map to a research question and a planned analysis. Avoid vague verbs like *to understand* or *to know*.", tool: ["method", "question", "Build my objectives"] },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Research questions vs hypotheses", k: ["research hypothesis", "hypotheses", "research questions and hypotheses", "write hypotheses"], a: "**Research questions** ask what you want to find out: \"What is the prevalence of hypertension among adults in Ho?\" **Hypotheses** predict a relationship you will test statistically: \"There is a significant association between salt intake and hypertension.\"\n\nDescriptive studies usually need only research questions. Analytical studies (comparing groups, looking for associations) state hypotheses, often as a null (H0: no association) and an alternative (H1: an association)." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Significance of the study", k: ["significance of the study", "justification", "rationale", "importance of the study"], a: "The **significance (justification)** explains **who benefits and how**:\n\n- **Policy makers / programme managers:** evidence to plan or improve services.\n- **Health workers, teachers or practitioners:** guidance for practice.\n- **Communities:** better services or awareness.\n- **Researchers:** fills a gap and provides a baseline for future studies.\n\nBe specific to your study; avoid generic claims that could apply to any topic." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Scope, delimitations and limitations", k: ["scope of the study", "delimitation", "delimitations", "limitations of the study", "limitation"], a: "**Scope / delimitations** are boundaries **you chose**: the place, population, time period and variables covered (e.g. adults aged 18+ in Ho Municipality, 2026).\n\n**Limitations** are weaknesses **outside your control** that affect the results, e.g. self-reported data (recall bias), cross-sectional design (no cause and effect), non-probability sampling (limited generalisability). For each limitation, say how you reduced its effect." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Operational definitions", k: ["operational definition", "operationalization", "operationalisation", "definition of terms", "definition of key terms"], a: "An **operational definition** states exactly how a concept is **measured** in your study, so anyone could repeat it.\n\nExample: *Hypertension* = systolic BP ≥ 140 mmHg and/or diastolic BP ≥ 90 mmHg, or current use of antihypertensive medication. *Physical activity* = at least 150 minutes of moderate activity per week (WHO).\n\nUse recognised cut-offs and cite their source." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Conceptual framework", k: ["conceptual framework", "conceptual model"], a: "A **conceptual framework** is a diagram (with a short explanation) of **the variables in your study and how you expect them to relate**: independent variables (e.g. socio-demographic, behavioural, health-system factors) pointing to the dependent variable (e.g. hypertension), sometimes with confounders or mediators.\n\nIt is usually built from the literature or adapted from a theory, and it guides your questionnaire and analysis: every box should become a variable you measure." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Theoretical framework", k: ["theoretical framework", "theory", "health belief model", "theory of planned behaviour", "theory of planned behavior", "socio-ecological model", "andersen model"], a: "A **theoretical framework** is an established theory that explains your phenomenon, which you use to guide the study. Common examples in health and social research:\n\n- **Health Belief Model:** perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, cues to action, self-efficacy.\n- **Theory of Planned Behaviour:** attitudes, subjective norms, perceived behavioural control lead to intention, then behaviour.\n- **Socio-ecological model:** individual, interpersonal, community, organisational and policy levels.\n- **Andersen's model of health service use:** predisposing, enabling and need factors.\n\nThe **conceptual framework** is often your own adaptation of a theory to your variables." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Writing the methodology chapter", k: ["methodology chapter", "chapter three", "chapter 3", "research methodology", "methods section", "write methodology"], a: "The **methodology (Chapter 3)** should let someone repeat your study. Typical sections:\n\n- **Study design** (e.g. analytical cross-sectional) and why it fits\n- **Study area / setting**\n- **Study population**, **inclusion and exclusion criteria**\n- **Sample size** (formula with all values) and **sampling technique** step by step\n- **Data collection tool** (questionnaire, its sections, source, pretesting) and **procedure**\n- **Variables**: dependent and independent, with operational definitions\n- **Validity and reliability** (pilot, Cronbach's alpha)\n- **Data management and analysis**: software, tests for each objective, significance level\n- **Ethical considerations**: approval, consent, confidentiality\n\nWrite it in the past tense in the final thesis (future tense in a proposal)." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Inclusion and exclusion criteria", k: ["inclusion criteria", "exclusion criteria", "eligibility criteria", "eligibility"], a: "**Inclusion criteria** define who is eligible (e.g. adults aged 18+, resident in Ho for at least 6 months, who consent). **Exclusion criteria** remove eligible people for whom participation would be unsafe, unreliable or bias the results (e.g. pregnant women for a BP study, people too ill to respond).\n\nExclusion criteria are not simply the opposite of inclusion criteria: \"aged under 18\" is not an exclusion criterion if inclusion already says 18+." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Data analysis plan", k: ["data analysis plan", "analysis plan", "plan of analysis", "how to analyse my data", "how to analyze my data", "statistical analysis plan"], a: "An **analysis plan** links each objective to a method:\n\n- **Describe** the sample: frequencies and percentages for categories; mean (SD) or median (IQR) for numbers.\n- **Prevalence / proportion** objectives: percentage with 95% CI.\n- **Comparisons:** t-test / ANOVA (numeric outcome), chi-square / Fisher's (categorical).\n- **Associated factors:** bivariable then multivariable regression (logistic or modified Poisson for yes/no outcomes, linear for numeric), reporting adjusted estimates with 95% CI.\n- State the **software** (e.g. Stata 17, SPSS 26, R) and **significance level** (p < 0.05).\n\nQuantAI's test selector suggests the right test for each objective.", tool: ["method", "test", "Choose a test"] },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Presenting results (Chapter 4)", k: ["results chapter", "chapter four", "chapter 4", "present results", "presenting results", "write results"], a: "In the **results chapter**, report findings **objective by objective**, without discussing them:\n\n- Start with the **response rate** and **participants' characteristics** (Table 1).\n- Use **tables** for detailed numbers and **figures** (bar charts, line graphs) for patterns; don't repeat every number in the text.\n- Number and title every table and figure; give n, percentages, means (SD) or medians (IQR).\n- For associations, report the **estimate, 95% CI and p-value**, e.g. \"aOR = 2.1, 95% CI: 1.3–3.4, p = 0.002\".\n\nSave interpretation and comparison with other studies for the discussion." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Writing the discussion", k: ["discussion chapter", "chapter five", "chapter 5", "write discussion", "discussion of findings", "discussion section", "the discussion", "discussion"], a: "A strong **discussion**, objective by objective:\n\n1. **State the main finding** in one sentence.\n2. **Compare** it with other studies (similar or different) and **explain** why it may agree or differ (setting, design, population, measurement).\n3. **Interpret** what it means in practice.\n\nThen add **strengths and limitations**, **conclusions** (answering each objective, no new results) and **recommendations** aimed at specific people (Ministry of Health, district health directorate, schools, future researchers)." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Recommendations", k: ["recommendations", "write recommendations", "recommendation"], a: "Good **recommendations** come directly from your findings and say **who should do what**:\n\n- *Ho Municipal Health Directorate* should introduce community BP screening, because 41% of hypertensive participants were unaware of their status.\n- *Future researchers* should use a cohort design to establish the direction of the association.\n\nAvoid recommendations that your results don't support." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Writing an abstract", k: ["abstract", "write an abstract", "executive summary"], a: "An **abstract** (usually 250–300 words) summarises the whole study:\n\n- **Background:** the problem in 1–2 sentences.\n- **Objective:** what you aimed to do.\n- **Methods:** design, setting, sample size, sampling, tools, analysis.\n- **Results:** key numbers (prevalence with 95% CI, main adjusted associations).\n- **Conclusion:** what it means and the main recommendation.\n\nWrite it last, use no citations, and make the numbers match your results exactly." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Referencing (APA and Vancouver)", k: ["referencing", "references", "citation", "citations", "apa style", "apa 7", "vancouver", "harvard referencing", "cite"], a: "Use one style consistently, as your department requires:\n\n- **APA 7th (author–date):** in text (Mensah & Owusu, 2023); list alphabetically. Journal format: Mensah, K., & Owusu, A. (2023). Title of article. *Journal Name, 12*(3), 45–56. https://doi.org/…\n- **Vancouver (numbered, common in health):** in text [1]; list numbered in order of citation: 1. Mensah K, Owusu A. Title of article. J Name. 2023;12(3):45–56.\n\nA reference manager (**Zotero**, free, or Mendeley) formats citations automatically from Word. Cite every source you use, including statistics and guidelines." },
{ c: "thesis", t: "Avoiding plagiarism", k: ["plagiarism", "paraphrase", "paraphrasing", "turnitin", "similarity index"], a: "**Plagiarism** is presenting other people's words or ideas as your own. Avoid it by:\n\n- **paraphrasing** properly: read, close the source, write the idea in your own words and structure, then cite it;\n- using **quotation marks** for exact words, sparingly;\n- citing **every** source, including data, figures and definitions;\n- keeping track of sources with a reference manager.\n\nUniversities check with tools like Turnitin; many accept a similarity index below about 15–20%, but check your institution's rule." },

/* ---------------- Design and sampling ---------------- */
{ c: "design", t: "Descriptive vs analytical studies", k: ["descriptive study", "analytical study", "descriptive vs analytical", "observational study", "observational vs experimental"], a: "**Descriptive studies** describe *what, who, where, when*: prevalence of a condition, characteristics of a group (e.g. a descriptive cross-sectional survey).\n\n**Analytical studies** test *why*: associations between exposures and outcomes, with a comparison group (cross-sectional analytical, case-control, cohort).\n\n**Observational** studies observe without intervening; **experimental** studies (trials) assign an intervention." },
{ c: "design", t: "Quasi-experimental study", k: ["quasi-experimental", "quasi experimental", "pre-post", "before and after study", "pretest posttest", "pre-test post-test"], a: "A **quasi-experimental study** tests an intervention **without random allocation**, e.g. comparing an intervention school with a similar control school, or measuring the same group before and after.\n\nIt is more practical than an RCT but more open to confounding. Strengthen it with a **control group**, **baseline measurements**, and analysis such as **difference-in-differences** (change in intervention group minus change in control group)." },
{ c: "design", t: "Ecological study", k: ["ecological study", "ecological fallacy"], a: "An **ecological study** uses **group-level data** (regions, districts, countries) rather than individuals, e.g. comparing malaria rates with rainfall across regions. It is quick and uses existing data.\n\nBeware the **ecological fallacy**: an association between groups may not hold for individuals." },
{ c: "design", t: "Study population vs target population", k: ["study population", "target population", "sampling frame", "source population", "accessible population"], a: "- **Target population:** everyone you want your results to apply to (e.g. all adults in Volta Region).\n- **Study (accessible) population:** those you can actually reach (e.g. adults in three communities in Ho).\n- **Sampling frame:** the actual list you select from (e.g. household register, class lists).\n- **Sample:** the people selected and studied.\n\nA poor sampling frame causes selection bias." },
{ c: "design", t: "Simple random sampling", k: ["simple random sampling", "random numbers", "lottery method"], a: "In **simple random sampling**, every member of the sampling frame has an equal chance of selection. Number everyone on the list, then choose numbers using a random number generator (Excel =RANDBETWEEN, or the lottery method).\n\nIt needs a complete list, so it is often used in the final stage of multistage sampling." },
{ c: "design", t: "Systematic sampling", k: ["systematic sampling", "sampling interval", "every kth"], a: "In **systematic sampling**, you pick every *k*th unit from a list or queue: **k = population size ÷ sample size**. Choose a random start between 1 and k, then add k each time.\n\nExample: 1,200 patients, sample of 300, k = 4; random start 3 gives 3, 7, 11… Useful for clinic attendees or households along a route. Avoid it if the list has a repeating pattern." },
{ c: "design", t: "Stratified sampling", k: ["stratified sampling", "strata", "proportionate allocation", "proportional allocation"], a: "In **stratified sampling**, you divide the population into groups (**strata**, e.g. year of study, sex, district) and randomly sample within each. With **proportionate allocation**, each stratum's sample matches its share of the population: n_stratum = (N_stratum ÷ N) × n.\n\nIt guarantees every group is represented and usually improves precision." },
{ c: "design", t: "Cluster and multistage sampling", k: ["cluster sampling", "multistage sampling", "multi-stage sampling", "design effect", "clusters"], a: "In **cluster sampling**, you randomly select whole groups (**clusters**: communities, schools, enumeration areas) and then study everyone or a sample within them. **Multistage sampling** repeats this in steps (districts, then communities, then households, then individuals), as in the Ghana DHS.\n\nPeople in the same cluster tend to be similar, so multiply your sample size by a **design effect** (often 1.5–2) and account for clustering in the analysis." },
{ c: "design", t: "Non-probability sampling", k: ["convenience sampling", "purposive sampling", "snowball sampling", "quota sampling", "non-probability sampling", "non probability sampling", "consecutive sampling"], a: "**Non-probability sampling** does not give everyone a known chance of selection:\n\n- **Convenience:** whoever is available (quick, but high risk of bias).\n- **Consecutive:** every eligible person attending in a period (better than convenience in clinics).\n- **Purposive:** chosen for specific knowledge or characteristics (common in qualitative work).\n- **Snowball:** participants recruit others (hard-to-reach groups).\n- **Quota:** fill set numbers per group.\n\nResults are less generalisable, so state this as a limitation." },
{ c: "design", t: "Sample size for a proportion (Cochran)", k: ["cochran", "cochran formula", "sample size formula", "sample size for proportion", "yamane", "taro yamane"], a: "For estimating a proportion, **Cochran's formula**: **n = Z² × p × (1 − p) / d²**\n\n- Z = 1.96 for 95% confidence\n- p = expected proportion from a previous study (use 0.5 if unknown, which gives the largest n)\n- d = margin of error (usually 0.05)\n\nExample: p = 0.30, d = 0.05 gives n = 1.96² × 0.3 × 0.7 / 0.05² ≈ 323. Then add for **non-response** (n ÷ (1 − rate), e.g. 10% gives 359) and multiply by the **design effect** for cluster samples. For a small known population, apply the finite population correction.\n\n**Yamane's** formula n = N / (1 + N·e²) is a simplified version that assumes p = 0.5; many reviewers prefer Cochran with a justified p.", tool: ["method", "sample", "Calculate my sample size"] },
{ c: "design", t: "Response rate and non-response", k: ["response rate", "non-response", "nonresponse", "attrition"], a: "**Response rate** = number who completed ÷ number eligible and approached × 100. Report it at the start of your results.\n\nPlan for **non-response** by inflating the sample size (n ÷ (1 − expected non-response)). High non-response can cause bias if non-responders differ from responders: compare their basic characteristics if you can." },
{ c: "design", t: "Blinding and allocation concealment", k: ["blinding", "double blind", "single blind", "allocation concealment", "placebo"], a: "**Blinding** means participants, staff or assessors don't know who received the intervention, which prevents expectations from biasing results (**single-blind**: participants; **double-blind**: participants and investigators).\n\n**Allocation concealment** hides the next assignment until a participant is enrolled (e.g. sealed opaque envelopes, central randomisation), so recruiters can't influence who goes where." },

/* ---------------- Measurement and data ---------------- */
{ c: "measure", t: "Cronbach's alpha", k: ["cronbach", "cronbach's alpha", "cronbach alpha", "internal consistency"], a: "**Cronbach's alpha** measures **internal consistency**: how well items in a scale measure the same thing (e.g. 10 knowledge questions). It ranges from 0 to 1:\n\n- ≥ 0.9 excellent, 0.8–0.9 good, **0.7–0.8 acceptable**, 0.6–0.7 questionable, < 0.6 poor.\n\nCalculate it on your pilot data for each scale (Stata: `alpha item1-item10`; SPSS: Analyze › Scale › Reliability Analysis). Very high values (> 0.95) may mean redundant items." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Likert scale analysis", k: ["likert", "likert scale", "agreement scale", "five point scale", "5-point scale"], a: "A **Likert item** (strongly disagree to strongly agree) is **ordinal**. Options:\n\n- **Single items:** report frequencies and percentages, or median (IQR). Compare groups with Mann–Whitney or chi-square.\n- **Scales (sum or mean of several items):** often treated as continuous (mean, SD, t-test, linear regression), especially with 5+ items and good Cronbach's alpha.\n- **Knowledge or attitude levels:** sum the scores and categorise with a stated cut-off (e.g. ≥ 70% = good knowledge, or Bloom's cut-off) and justify it." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Knowledge, attitude and practice scoring", k: ["kap study", "knowledge attitude practice", "knowledge score", "scoring knowledge", "bloom's cut-off", "blooms cut off", "good knowledge"], a: "In **KAP studies**, score each correct knowledge answer 1 (wrong or don't know = 0), sum the scores, and convert to a percentage. Common classifications:\n\n- **Bloom's cut-off:** 80–100% good, 60–79% moderate, < 60% poor.\n- Or **mean/median split:** above the mean = good knowledge.\n\nState your method in the methodology chapter, cite its source, and apply the same approach to attitudes and practices." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Coding and data entry", k: ["coding", "data entry", "codebook", "code my data", "data coding", "enter data", "data management"], a: "Good **data management** saves weeks later:\n\n- Create a **codebook**: variable name, label, codes (1 = Male, 2 = Female), units, missing-value code.\n- Enter data in a structured tool (**KoboToolbox**, **ODK** or Google Forms for collection; Excel or EpiData for entry) with one row per participant and one column per variable.\n- Use short variable names without spaces (e.g. sys_bp) and numbers for categories.\n- Double-enter a sample or run range checks, keep a backup, and remove names or phone numbers (keep only an ID).\n\nQuantAI's data check flags impossible values, duplicates and missing-value codes automatically." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Data cleaning", k: ["data cleaning", "clean my data", "cleaning data", "outliers", "outlier", "impossible values"], a: "**Data cleaning** before analysis:\n\n- **Duplicates:** remove repeated IDs.\n- **Range checks:** impossible values (age 250, BMI 7).\n- **Consistency checks:** e.g. males marked pregnant.\n- **Missing-value codes** like 99 or 999: set them to missing.\n- **Outliers:** check against the source. Correct errors, but don't delete real extreme values just because they're unusual; consider robust methods instead.\n- Keep a **log** of every change.\n\nAttach your file in QuantAI and say \"check my data for problems\"." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Missing data", k: ["missing data", "missing values", "multiple imputation", "imputation", "mcar", "mar", "mnar"], a: "**Missing data** can bias results. The three types:\n\n- **MCAR (completely at random):** unrelated to anything; analysing complete cases is unbiased but loses power.\n- **MAR (at random):** related to observed variables; **multiple imputation** is the standard fix.\n- **MNAR (not at random):** related to the missing value itself; hard to fix, so discuss as a limitation and do sensitivity analyses.\n\nAlways report how much is missing for each variable and how you handled it. Avoid replacing missing values with the mean for main analyses." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Recoding and categorising variables", k: ["recode", "recoding", "categorise", "categorize", "age groups", "create categories", "dummy variables", "dummy variable"], a: "**Recoding** turns raw values into analysis-ready groups, e.g. age into 18–29, 30–44, 45–59, 60+, or BMI into WHO categories. Use **recognised cut-offs** where they exist and enough people per group.\n\nCategorising a continuous variable loses information, so keep it continuous in regression if the relationship is roughly linear. For categorical predictors in regression, software creates **dummy variables** with one **reference category** (choose a meaningful one, e.g. the lowest risk or largest group)." },
{ c: "measure", t: "Levels of measurement", k: ["levels of measurement", "nominal ordinal interval ratio", "interval scale", "ratio scale", "scale of measurement"], a: "The four **levels of measurement**:\n\n- **Nominal:** categories without order (religion, region).\n- **Ordinal:** ordered categories (education level, Likert responses).\n- **Interval:** equal spacing, no true zero (temperature in °C).\n- **Ratio:** equal spacing with a true zero (age, weight, income).\n\nInterval and ratio are both treated as **continuous** in analysis." },

/* ---------------- Statistics basics ---------------- */
{ c: "stats", t: "Descriptive vs inferential statistics", k: ["descriptive statistics", "inferential statistics", "descriptive vs inferential"], a: "**Descriptive statistics** summarise your sample: counts, percentages, means, medians, SDs, tables and charts.\n\n**Inferential statistics** use the sample to draw conclusions about the population: confidence intervals, hypothesis tests (t-test, chi-square) and regression models, with p-values.\n\nMost theses use both: descriptive for Table 1 and the prevalence; inferential for associations." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Standard error vs standard deviation", k: ["standard error", "sem", "standard error of the mean", "standard error vs standard deviation"], a: "The **standard deviation (SD)** describes how spread out individual values are. The **standard error (SE)** describes how precise your estimate of the mean is: **SE = SD ÷ √n**.\n\nUse **SD** to describe your sample (mean ± SD) and the **SE** (or better, a 95% CI) to show precision. The SE shrinks as the sample grows; the SD doesn't." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Effect size", k: ["effect size", "cohen's d", "cohens d", "cramer's v", "eta squared", "magnitude of effect"], a: "An **effect size** shows **how big** a difference or association is, independent of sample size:\n\n- **Cohen's d** (difference in means ÷ pooled SD): 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large.\n- **Cramér's V** (chi-square): about 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large (for 2×2 tables).\n- **Eta squared** (ANOVA), **r** (correlation), **odds/risk ratios** (binary outcomes).\n\nReport effect sizes with confidence intervals alongside p-values." },
{ c: "stats", t: "One-tailed vs two-tailed tests", k: ["one-tailed", "two-tailed", "one tailed", "two tailed", "one-sided", "two-sided"], a: "A **two-tailed test** checks for a difference in **either direction**; a **one-tailed test** checks only one direction (e.g. drug A is *better* than B).\n\nUse **two-tailed** tests by default. One-tailed tests are only justified when a difference in the other direction is impossible or irrelevant and this was decided before seeing the data." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Parametric vs non-parametric", k: ["parametric", "parametric vs non-parametric", "parametric test"], a: "**Parametric tests** (t-test, ANOVA, Pearson correlation, linear regression) assume the data follow a distribution, usually normal, and compare means. **Non-parametric tests** (Mann–Whitney, Kruskal–Wallis, Spearman) use ranks and compare medians or distributions.\n\nUse parametric tests when assumptions hold or samples are large (thanks to the central limit theorem); otherwise use non-parametric. QuantAI checks the assumptions and chooses for you." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Central limit theorem", k: ["central limit theorem", "clt"], a: "The **central limit theorem** says that the distribution of **sample means** is approximately normal when the sample is large enough (often n ≥ 30 per group), even if the data themselves are not normal.\n\nThat's why t-tests and confidence intervals for means work reasonably with large samples of skewed data. It doesn't fix very heavy skew or outliers in small samples." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Multiple comparisons and post-hoc tests", k: ["multiple comparisons", "post-hoc", "post hoc", "bonferroni", "tukey"], a: "Running many tests raises the chance of a false positive. After a significant ANOVA, **post-hoc tests** compare pairs of groups while controlling this:\n\n- **Tukey's HSD:** all pairwise comparisons, equal variances.\n- **Games–Howell:** unequal variances.\n- **Bonferroni:** divide the significance level by the number of tests (e.g. 0.05 ÷ 3 = 0.017).\n- **Dunn's test** after Kruskal–Wallis." },
{ c: "stats", t: "McNemar's test", k: ["mcnemar", "mcnemar's test", "paired proportions"], a: "**McNemar's test** compares **paired binary outcomes**: the same people measured twice (aware of status before vs after an intervention), or matched pairs. It uses only the people who changed category.\n\nDon't use an ordinary chi-square test for paired data. QuantAI's Compare tool runs McNemar when you tick \"paired\"." },
{ c: "stats", t: "Repeated measures", k: ["repeated measures", "repeated-measures anova", "longitudinal data", "multiple time points"], a: "**Repeated measures** data have the same people measured several times (e.g. BP at baseline, 3 and 6 months). Observations from the same person are correlated, so ordinary tests are wrong.\n\nOptions: **repeated-measures ANOVA** (complete, balanced data), **Friedman test** (non-parametric), or better, **linear mixed-effects models** which handle missing time points. QuantAI's regression tool fits mixed models with a cluster (person) variable." },

/* ---------------- Regression ---------------- */
{ c: "regression", t: "Bivariable vs multivariable analysis", k: ["bivariate", "bivariable", "multivariable", "multivariate", "crude and adjusted", "crude vs adjusted", "adjusted odds ratio", "aor", "cor"], a: "**Bivariable (crude)** analysis looks at each factor with the outcome on its own. **Multivariable (adjusted)** analysis puts several factors into one model, so each estimate is adjusted for the others (controls confounding).\n\nCommon approach: run crude analyses, take factors with p < 0.2 (or those known from the literature) into the multivariable model, and report **adjusted** estimates (aOR or aPR) with 95% CI. \"Multivariate\" strictly means several *outcomes*; for one outcome with many predictors, say \"multivariable\"." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Regression assumptions and diagnostics", k: ["regression assumptions", "assumptions of regression", "residuals", "homoscedasticity", "heteroscedasticity", "regression diagnostics"], a: "Check these after fitting a **linear regression**:\n\n- **Linearity:** residuals vs fitted values show no pattern.\n- **Normality of residuals:** histogram or Q-Q plot.\n- **Equal variance (homoscedasticity):** spread of residuals is constant.\n- **Independence:** no clustering or repeated measures (otherwise use mixed models).\n- **No strong multicollinearity:** VIF < 5–10.\n- **Influential points:** Cook's distance.\n\nFor **logistic regression**: check multicollinearity, enough events per variable (about 10), and goodness of fit (Hosmer–Lemeshow)." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Multicollinearity (VIF)", k: ["multicollinearity", "collinearity", "vif", "variance inflation factor"], a: "**Multicollinearity** happens when predictors are strongly related to each other (e.g. weight and BMI), which makes estimates unstable and standard errors large.\n\nCheck the **variance inflation factor (VIF)**: below 5 is fine; above 10 is a serious problem. Fix it by dropping one of the related variables, combining them, or choosing the most meaningful one." },
{ c: "regression", t: "R-squared", k: ["r squared", "r-squared", "r2", "adjusted r squared", "coefficient of determination"], a: "**R²** is the proportion of variation in the outcome explained by the model (0 to 1; 0.30 means 30%). **Adjusted R²** penalises extra predictors, so use it to compare models with different numbers of variables.\n\nA low R² doesn't make a model useless: in health and social research, individual behaviour is hard to predict, but associations can still be important." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Interaction and effect modification", k: ["interaction", "effect modification", "effect modifier", "moderator", "moderation", "interaction term"], a: "**Effect modification (interaction)** means the effect of an exposure **differs across levels of another variable**, e.g. salt intake raises BP more in older people.\n\nTest it by adding an **interaction term** (exposure × modifier) to the regression; if significant, report effects separately for each group. Unlike confounding, effect modification is a real finding to describe, not a bias to remove." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Mediation", k: ["mediation", "mediator", "mediating variable", "indirect effect"], a: "A **mediator** lies on the **causal pathway** between exposure and outcome: education → income → access to care. Adjusting for a mediator removes part of the effect you want to measure, so don't adjust for mediators when estimating the total effect.\n\nFormal mediation analysis splits the total effect into **direct** and **indirect** effects (e.g. with Stata's `medeff` or R's `mediation` package)." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Poisson and negative binomial regression", k: ["poisson regression", "negative binomial", "count data", "incidence rate ratio", "irr", "overdispersion"], a: "**Poisson regression** models **counts** (number of clinic visits, malaria episodes) and gives **incidence rate ratios (IRR)**. Include an **offset** (log of time at risk or population) when exposure time differs.\n\nIf the variance is much larger than the mean (**overdispersion**), use **negative binomial regression**. **Modified Poisson** (with robust errors) is used for binary outcomes to get prevalence or risk ratios." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Ordinal and multinomial regression", k: ["ordinal regression", "ordinal logistic", "proportional odds", "multinomial regression", "multinomial logistic"], a: "- **Ordinal logistic regression** is for ordered outcomes (poor / moderate / good knowledge). It assumes **proportional odds**: the effect of each predictor is the same across cut-points (check with the Brant test).\n- **Multinomial logistic regression** is for unordered outcomes with 3+ categories (choice of health facility: public / private / traditional), giving a relative risk ratio for each category vs a reference.\n\nQuantAI's regression tool fits both." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Mixed-effects (multilevel) models", k: ["mixed effects", "mixed-effects", "multilevel", "multi-level", "hierarchical model", "random effects", "random intercept", "icc"], a: "**Multilevel (mixed-effects) models** handle data with a **hierarchy**: pupils within schools, patients within clinics, repeated visits within people. A **random intercept** lets each cluster have its own baseline, which corrects standard errors for clustering.\n\nThe **intraclass correlation (ICC)** shows how much of the variation is between clusters. Use these models when observations are not independent, e.g. DHS data (women within clusters) or repeated measurements." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Model building and variable selection", k: ["model building", "variable selection", "stepwise", "backward elimination", "forward selection", "which variables to include"], a: "Choose variables for a multivariable model using **theory and the literature first**, then statistics:\n\n- Include known **confounders** and your main exposures.\n- A common screen is **p < 0.2** (or 0.25) in bivariable analysis.\n- Avoid relying only on automatic **stepwise** selection: it overfits and gives biased p-values.\n- Keep about **10 outcome events per variable** in logistic regression.\n- Don't adjust for mediators or variables caused by the outcome." },
{ c: "regression", t: "Goodness of fit and ROC curve", k: ["goodness of fit", "hosmer", "hosmer-lemeshow", "roc curve", "auc", "area under the curve", "c-statistic", "model fit", "aic", "bic"], a: "For **logistic regression**:\n\n- **Hosmer–Lemeshow test:** p > 0.05 suggests the model fits adequately.\n- **ROC curve / AUC (c-statistic):** how well the model separates people with and without the outcome. 0.5 = no better than chance; 0.7–0.8 acceptable; 0.8–0.9 excellent.\n\nTo compare models, a lower **AIC** or **BIC** indicates a better balance of fit and simplicity." },

/* ---------------- Epidemiology ---------------- */
{ c: "epi", t: "Measures of disease frequency", k: ["measures of disease frequency", "incidence rate", "cumulative incidence", "attack rate", "point prevalence", "period prevalence", "person-time", "person years"], a: "- **Prevalence** = existing cases ÷ population at a time point (point) or over a period (period prevalence).\n- **Cumulative incidence (risk)** = new cases ÷ population at risk at the start, over a period.\n- **Incidence rate** = new cases ÷ total **person-time** at risk (e.g. 12 per 1,000 person-years).\n- **Attack rate** = cumulative incidence during an outbreak.\n\nPrevalence ≈ incidence × average duration of disease." },
{ c: "epi", t: "Mortality and health indicators", k: ["mortality rate", "crude death rate", "infant mortality", "maternal mortality", "under-five mortality", "under five mortality", "case fatality", "neonatal mortality"], a: "Common indicators:\n\n- **Crude death rate:** deaths per 1,000 population per year.\n- **Infant mortality rate:** deaths under age 1 per 1,000 live births.\n- **Neonatal mortality rate:** deaths in the first 28 days per 1,000 live births.\n- **Under-five mortality rate:** deaths before age 5 per 1,000 live births.\n- **Maternal mortality ratio:** maternal deaths per 100,000 live births.\n- **Case fatality rate:** deaths among cases ÷ number of cases × 100.\n\nGhana's latest figures are in the Ghana DHS and Ghana Health Service reports." },
{ c: "epi", t: "Attributable risk and NNT", k: ["attributable risk", "risk difference", "population attributable", "number needed to treat", "nnt", "absolute risk reduction"], a: "- **Risk difference (attributable risk)** = risk in exposed − risk in unexposed: the extra risk due to the exposure.\n- **Population attributable fraction** = the share of all cases in the population due to the exposure (useful for planning).\n- **Absolute risk reduction (ARR)** = control risk − treated risk; **number needed to treat (NNT)** = 1 ÷ ARR.\n\nExample: risk 10% untreated vs 6% treated gives ARR = 4% and NNT = 25." },
{ c: "epi", t: "Causation (Bradford Hill)", k: ["causation", "causality", "bradford hill", "cause and effect", "causal inference"], a: "An association isn't proof of cause. The **Bradford Hill considerations** help judge causation:\n\n- **Strength** of association\n- **Consistency** across studies\n- **Specificity**\n- **Temporality:** the cause comes before the effect (essential)\n- **Biological gradient** (dose–response)\n- **Plausibility** and **coherence** with existing knowledge\n- **Experiment:** removing the exposure reduces the outcome\n- **Analogy**\n\nCross-sectional studies can't establish temporality, so describe their findings as associations." },
{ c: "epi", t: "Survey weights and complex samples (DHS)", k: ["survey weights", "sampling weights", "weighting", "complex survey", "dhs data", "svyset", "demographic and health survey", "mics"], a: "Surveys like the **Ghana DHS** and **MICS** use stratified multistage cluster sampling, so some groups are over-sampled. To get correct national estimates and standard errors, analyse with the survey design:\n\n- Apply the **weight** (in DHS, v005 ÷ 1,000,000 for women),\n- declare **clusters** (v021) and **strata** (v022 or v023).\n\nIn Stata: `svyset v021 [pw=wt], strata(v022)` then use `svy:` before commands. Download DHS data free (with registration) from dhsprogram.com." },
{ c: "epi", t: "Stratified analysis (Mantel-Haenszel)", k: ["stratified analysis", "mantel-haenszel", "mantel haenszel", "stratification"], a: "**Stratified analysis** checks an association within levels of a third variable (e.g. salt and hypertension separately in under-40s and over-40s).\n\n- If the stratum-specific estimates are similar but differ from the crude estimate, the third variable is a **confounder**: report the **Mantel–Haenszel** pooled estimate.\n- If the stratum estimates differ from each other, it is **effect modification**: report them separately." },

/* ---------------- Diagnostic tests and agreement ---------------- */
{ c: "diagnostic", t: "Sensitivity and specificity", k: ["sensitivity", "specificity", "positive predictive value", "negative predictive value", "ppv", "npv", "diagnostic accuracy", "screening test"], a: "Compared with a **gold standard**:\n\n- **Sensitivity** = true positives ÷ all who have the disease (how well the test catches cases).\n- **Specificity** = true negatives ÷ all without the disease (how well it rules out non-cases).\n- **PPV** = true positives ÷ all who test positive.\n- **NPV** = true negatives ÷ all who test negative.\n\nSensitivity and specificity are properties of the test; PPV and NPV depend on how common the disease is. Report diagnostic studies with the **STARD** checklist." },
{ c: "diagnostic", t: "Agreement: kappa and ICC", k: ["kappa", "cohen's kappa", "inter-rater", "interrater", "inter-observer", "agreement", "intraclass correlation", "bland-altman", "bland altman"], a: "- **Cohen's kappa** measures agreement between two raters on **categories**, beyond chance: < 0.2 slight, 0.21–0.40 fair, 0.41–0.60 moderate, 0.61–0.80 substantial, > 0.80 almost perfect.\n- **ICC (intraclass correlation)** measures agreement for **continuous** measurements (e.g. two nurses measuring BP).\n- **Bland–Altman plots** show the bias and limits of agreement between two measurement methods.\n\nDon't use correlation to measure agreement: two methods can be highly correlated but still disagree." },

/* ---------------- Qualitative ---------------- */
{ c: "qual", t: "Qualitative data collection", k: ["in-depth interview", "in depth interview", "focus group", "fgd", "key informant", "interview guide", "observation"], a: "Main **qualitative methods**:\n\n- **In-depth interviews (IDIs):** one-to-one, for personal experiences and sensitive topics.\n- **Focus group discussions (FGDs):** 6–10 similar participants, for shared views and norms. A moderator guides, and a note-taker records.\n- **Key informant interviews:** people with special knowledge (nurses, chiefs, programme officers).\n- **Observation:** watching practices in their setting.\n\nUse a semi-structured **interview guide** with open questions and probes, record with consent, and transcribe verbatim (translating if conducted in Twi, Ewe or another language)." },
{ c: "qual", t: "Thematic analysis", k: ["thematic analysis", "themes", "coding qualitative", "qualitative analysis", "braun and clarke", "nvivo", "atlas.ti"], a: "**Thematic analysis** (Braun & Clarke) has six phases:\n\n1. **Familiarise** yourself with the data (read transcripts several times).\n2. **Generate initial codes** (label meaningful segments).\n3. **Search for themes** (group related codes).\n4. **Review themes** against the data.\n5. **Define and name** themes.\n6. **Write up**, with quotes from participants as evidence (e.g. \"…\" Female, 34, FGD 2).\n\nSoftware such as **NVivo**, **ATLAS.ti** or free **Taguette** helps organise codes." },
{ c: "qual", t: "Saturation and qualitative sample size", k: ["saturation", "data saturation", "qualitative sample size", "how many interviews"], a: "Qualitative studies don't use sample size formulas. You keep collecting data until **saturation**: new interviews add no new codes or themes.\n\nAs a guide, saturation for fairly similar participants often comes after about **12–20 interviews** or **3–6 focus groups** per group, but justify it from your data and the literature." },
{ c: "qual", t: "Trustworthiness in qualitative research", k: ["trustworthiness", "credibility", "transferability", "dependability", "confirmability", "rigour", "rigor", "reflexivity", "member checking", "triangulation"], a: "Qualitative quality is judged by **trustworthiness** (Lincoln & Guba):\n\n- **Credibility:** member checking, triangulation, prolonged engagement.\n- **Transferability:** thick description of the setting and participants.\n- **Dependability:** an audit trail of decisions.\n- **Confirmability:** reflexivity (acknowledging your own influence), quotes that support themes.\n\n**Triangulation** means combining data sources, methods or researchers to strengthen conclusions. Report qualitative studies with the **COREQ** checklist." },

/* ---------------- Software ---------------- */
{ c: "software", t: "Which statistics software to use", k: ["which software", "spss or stata", "stata or spss", "statistical software", "spss vs stata", "r or stata", "software for analysis"], a: "- **SPSS:** menu-driven, easy for beginners; common in social sciences and nursing.\n- **Stata:** strong for epidemiology, survey (DHS) data and regression; commands make work reproducible; widely used in public health.\n- **R (with RStudio):** free and very powerful, with a steeper learning curve.\n- **Python:** free, good for data science and automation.\n- **QuantAI:** free in your browser, and it exports your analysis as Stata, SPSS, R and Python code.\n\nUse whatever your supervisor supports, and save your commands or syntax so your analysis can be repeated." },
{ c: "software", t: "Stata basics", k: ["stata", "stata commands", "how to use stata", "learn stata", "do-file", "do file"], a: "Key **Stata** commands:\n\n- `import excel \"data.xlsx\", firstrow clear` (or `import delimited` for CSV)\n- `describe`, `codebook`, `summarize age, detail`\n- `tab sex`, `tab sex hypertension, row chi2`\n- `ttest sbp, by(sex)`, `ranksum sbp, by(sex)`\n- `logistic hypertension age i.sex i.salt` (odds ratios)\n- `glm hypertension age i.sex, family(poisson) link(log) vce(robust) eform` (prevalence ratios)\n- `regress sbp age bmi i.sex`\n\nWrite commands in a **do-file** so your analysis is reproducible. QuantAI gives you the Stata code for every analysis it runs." },
{ c: "software", t: "SPSS basics", k: ["spss", "how to use spss", "learn spss", "spss syntax"], a: "Common **SPSS** menus:\n\n- Descriptives: **Analyze › Descriptive Statistics › Frequencies / Descriptives / Crosstabs** (tick Chi-square under Statistics).\n- t-test: **Analyze › Compare Means › Independent-Samples T Test**.\n- ANOVA: **Analyze › Compare Means › One-Way ANOVA**.\n- Non-parametric: **Analyze › Nonparametric Tests**.\n- Logistic regression: **Analyze › Regression › Binary Logistic**.\n- Reliability: **Analyze › Scale › Reliability Analysis**.\n\nClick **Paste** instead of OK to save the syntax, so you can rerun everything. QuantAI also gives SPSS syntax for its analyses." },
{ c: "software", t: "R basics", k: ["r programming", "rstudio", "learn r", "how to use r", "r software", "r code"], a: "Getting started with **R**: install **R** and **RStudio** (both free). A first script:\n\ndata <- read.csv(\"data.csv\")\nsummary(data)\ntable(data$sex, data$hypertension)\nchisq.test(table(data$sex, data$hypertension))\nt.test(sbp ~ sex, data = data)\nm <- glm(hypertension ~ age + sex, family = binomial, data = data)\nexp(cbind(OR = coef(m), confint(m)))\n\nUseful packages: **tidyverse** (data handling), **gtsummary** (Table 1 and regression tables), **survey** (DHS data). QuantAI exports R code for each analysis." },
{ c: "software", t: "Data collection tools (Kobo, ODK)", k: ["kobotoolbox", "kobo", "odk", "open data kit", "google forms", "data collection tool", "electronic data collection", "redcap"], a: "Electronic data collection reduces entry errors:\n\n- **KoboToolbox:** free, works offline on Android phones, popular in Ghana for surveys; exports to Excel, SPSS and CSV.\n- **ODK (Open Data Kit):** similar, powerful skip logic and validation.\n- **Google Forms:** simple online surveys (needs internet; weaker validation).\n- **REDCap:** secure, used by many health research institutions.\n\nBuild in **validation rules** (age 15–100), **skip logic** and **required fields**, and pilot the form before fieldwork." },

/* ---------------- Reporting ---------------- */
{ c: "reporting", t: "Reporting statistics (APA style)", k: ["report statistics", "apa format results", "how to report", "report p value", "report a t-test", "report chi square", "how to write results"], a: "Report the **test, statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value and effect size**:\n\n- t-test: *t*(418) = 3.21, *p* = .001, *d* = 0.31\n- Chi-square: χ²(1, *N* = 420) = 6.84, *p* = .009\n- Correlation: *r*(98) = .42, *p* < .001\n- Regression: aOR = 2.10, 95% CI [1.30, 3.40], *p* = .002\n\nGive exact p-values (p = .032), except below .001 (p < .001). QuantAI writes an APA-style sentence for every analysis that you can copy." },
{ c: "reporting", t: "Tables and figures", k: ["tables and figures", "how to present tables", "charts", "bar chart", "pie chart", "histogram", "graphs", "which chart"], a: "Choose the display for the data:\n\n- **Bar chart:** compare categories (better than pie charts for most purposes).\n- **Histogram:** distribution of a continuous variable.\n- **Box plot:** compare a continuous variable across groups.\n- **Line graph:** trends over time.\n- **Scatter plot:** relationship between two continuous variables.\n\nEvery table and figure needs a number, a clear title, labelled axes with units, the n, and a source or note. Table titles usually go above the table and figure titles below it (check your guidelines)." },
{ c: "reporting", t: "Table 1 (participant characteristics)", k: ["table 1", "socio-demographic table", "sociodemographic characteristics", "background characteristics", "demographic table"], a: "**Table 1** describes your participants: age, sex, education, occupation, income and other background variables.\n\n- Categories: n (%).\n- Continuous: mean (SD) if roughly normal, otherwise median (IQR).\n- Optionally split by the outcome or exposure group, with p-values from chi-square or t-tests.\n\nIn QuantAI, type \"describe my data\" or \"describe my data by sex\" to get Table 1." },
{ c: "reporting", t: "Interpreting an odds ratio in words", k: ["interpret odds ratio", "interpret aor", "interpreting odds ratios", "odds ratio interpretation", "interpret prevalence ratio"], a: "Interpretation templates:\n\n- **aOR = 2.10 (95% CI 1.30–3.40):** \"Participants with high salt intake had **2.1 times the odds** of hypertension compared with those with low intake, after adjusting for age, sex and BMI.\"\n- **aOR = 0.60:** \"…had **40% lower odds** of…\" (1 − 0.60 = 0.40).\n- **aPR = 1.35:** \"The prevalence of hypertension was **35% higher** among…\"\n\nIf the 95% CI includes 1, the association is not statistically significant. Avoid saying \"times more likely\" for odds ratios when the outcome is common; that wording fits risk or prevalence ratios." },
{ c: "reporting", t: "Reporting guidelines (EQUATOR)", k: ["reporting guidelines", "equator network", "equator", "spirit", "care guidelines", "tripod", "squire", "cheers"], a: "Reporting guidelines list what a paper or thesis must include for each design (all at equator-network.org):\n\n- **STROBE:** observational (cross-sectional, cohort, case-control)\n- **CONSORT:** randomised trials; **SPIRIT:** trial protocols\n- **STARD:** diagnostic accuracy\n- **COREQ / SRQR:** qualitative research\n- **PRISMA:** systematic reviews and meta-analyses\n- **TRIPOD:** prediction models; **CARE:** case reports\n\nQuantAI has interactive checklists for the main ones.", tool: ["method", "checklist", "Open the reporting checklists"] },

/* ---------------- Reviews and ethics ---------------- */
{ c: "review", t: "Searching the literature", k: ["literature search", "search strategy", "pubmed", "google scholar", "databases", "boolean", "keywords for search", "find articles", "mesh"], a: "A good **literature search**:\n\n- Use databases: **PubMed** (health), **Google Scholar**, **African Journals Online (AJOL)**, **Scopus**, **HINARI** (free access for Ghanaian institutions).\n- Build search terms from your question (e.g. PICO), with synonyms and **MeSH** terms.\n- Combine with **Boolean operators**: AND (narrows), OR (broadens synonyms), NOT; use \"quotation marks\" for phrases and * for word endings (hypertens*).\n- Filter by date (usually the last 5–10 years), language and study type.\n- Save sources in **Zotero** or Mendeley as you go." },
{ c: "review", t: "Critical appraisal", k: ["critical appraisal", "appraise a paper", "quality of studies", "casp", "risk of bias", "jbi checklist", "newcastle-ottawa", "newcastle ottawa"], a: "**Critical appraisal** judges whether a study's results can be trusted:\n\n- Was the **question** clear?\n- Was the **design** appropriate?\n- Was the **sample** representative and large enough?\n- Were measurements **valid and reliable**?\n- Was **confounding and bias** handled?\n- Are the **results** precise (CIs) and do the conclusions follow?\n\nUse tools such as **CASP** checklists, **JBI** checklists, the **Newcastle–Ottawa Scale** (observational studies) or **Cochrane Risk of Bias** (trials)." },
{ c: "ethics", t: "Ethics review in Ghana", k: ["ethics committee", "ethics review committee", "ghs-erc", "ghs erc", "ethical approval in ghana", "irb ghana", "noguchi ethics", "institutional review board"], a: "In Ghana, studies with human participants need approval before data collection, usually from one of:\n\n- your **university's ethics committee** (e.g. UHAS, KNUST CHRPE, UG College of Health Sciences),\n- the **Ghana Health Service Ethics Review Committee (GHS-ERC)** for studies in GHS facilities,\n- the **Noguchi Memorial Institute IRB**.\n\nYou'll typically submit the proposal, consent forms (in local languages if needed), data collection tools and an introductory letter. Also get **permission** from the facility, district or community leaders. Check each committee's current fees and timelines." },
{ c: "ethics", t: "Confidentiality and data protection", k: ["confidentiality", "anonymity", "data protection", "privacy", "anonymous", "data protection act"], a: "- **Anonymity:** even the researcher can't link responses to a person (e.g. no names collected).\n- **Confidentiality:** the researcher knows who participated but protects that information.\n\nGood practice: use **ID numbers instead of names**, store consent forms separately from data, password-protect files, limit access, and report only grouped results. Ghana's **Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843)** governs personal data." },
{ c: "ethics", t: "Consent with minors and vulnerable groups", k: ["assent", "minors", "children consent", "parental consent", "vulnerable groups", "vulnerable population"], a: "For participants under 18, obtain **parental or guardian consent** plus the child's own **assent** (agreement in age-appropriate language). Extra protections apply to other **vulnerable groups**: prisoners, people with cognitive impairment, patients in dependent relationships with staff.\n\nUse a witness and thumbprint for participants who can't read or write, and translate consent forms into the local language." },
];
